  <p>
    These measured values are then normalized by the equivalent torsional wheel
    rate and used in the following section to determine the minimum monocoque
    stiffness required for the next vehicle.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Normalized Stiffness Values</h2>

  <p>
    To make the results independent of the absolute wheel rate, each stiffness
    is divided by the equivalent torsional wheel rate:
  </p>

  \[
  K_i'
  =
  \frac{K_i}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}}
  \]

  <p>
    By definition:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{wheel}}' = 1
  \]

  <h3>Normalized Monocoque Stiffness</h3>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono}}'
  =
  \frac{3364}{171.92}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono}}'
  \approx
  19.57
  }
  \]

  <h3>Normalized Suspension Structural Stiffness</h3>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}'
  =
  \frac{4403}{171.92}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}'
  \approx
  25.61
  }
  \]

  <p>
    The measured structural stiffness values were therefore:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono}}' = 19.57,
  \qquad
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}' = 25.61
  }
  \]

  <p>
    In physical terms, the monocoque was approximately 19.6 times as stiff as
    the equivalent wheel rate, while the suspension structure was approximately
    25.6 times as stiff.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Determining the Monocoque Stiffness Target</h2>

  <p>
    The design objective is for 90% of the total vehicle compliance during the
    one-wheel-bump loading case to come from the suspension springs, represented
    by the equivalent torsional wheel rate. The remaining 10% of vehicle compliance
    is allowed to come from deformation of the monocoque and suspension structure.
  </p>

  <p>
    Compliance is defined as the inverse of stiffness:
  </p>

  \[
  C = \frac{1}{K}
  \]

  <p>
    Therefore, the target compliance distribution can be written as:
  </p>

  \[
  C_{\mathrm{wheel}}
  =
  0.90C_{\mathrm{vehicle}}
  \]

  \[
  C_{\mathrm{structure}}
  =
  C_{\mathrm{mono}}
  +
  C_{\mathrm{susp}}
  =
  0.10C_{\mathrm{vehicle}}
  \]

  <p>
    Taking the ratio of wheel compliance to combined structural compliance:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{C_{\mathrm{wheel}}}
  {C_{\mathrm{structure}}}
  =
  \frac{0.90}{0.10}
  =
  9
  \]

  <p>
    Because compliance is the inverse of stiffness:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{
  1/K_{\mathrm{wheel}}
  }{
  1/K_{\mathrm{structure}}
  }
  =
  9
  \]

  <p>
    Therefore:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{structure}}
  =
  9K_{\mathrm{wheel}}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    This means that the monocoque and suspension structure, acting together,
    must be approximately nine times stiffer than the equivalent torsional wheel
    rate. The required structural stiffness is then divided between the measured
    suspension structural stiffness and the required monocoque stiffness.
  </p>

  <h3>Normalized Vehicle Target</h3>

  <p>
    The same requirement can be expressed using the normalized stiffness model.
    The ideal rigid-structure vehicle has no compliance in either the monocoque
    or the suspension structure. Its normalized stiffness is therefore controlled
    entirely by the wheel rate:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{vehicle,rigid}}' = 1
  \]

  <p>
    Requiring 90% of total vehicle compliance to occur in the suspension springs
    is equivalent to requiring the actual vehicle to retain 90% of the stiffness
    of this ideal rigid-structure case:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{vehicle,target}}' = 0.90
  \]

  <p>
    The normalized series-stiffness equation is:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{vehicle}}'}
  =
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}'}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}'}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono}}'}
  \]

  <p>
    Since the wheel rate is normalized to one:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{wheel}}' = 1
  \]

  <p>
    the total normalized vehicle compliance at the 90% stiffness target is:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{vehicle,target}}'}
  =
  \frac{1}{0.90}
  =
  1.1111
  \]

  <p>
    The normalized wheel compliance is:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{wheel}}'}
  =
  1
  \]

  <p>
    Therefore, the compliance available to the monocoque and suspension
    structure combined is:
  </p>

  \[
  1.1111 - 1
  =
  0.1111
  \]

  <p>
    Thus:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{susp}}'}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'}
  =
  0.1111
  \]

  <p>
    Using the measured normalized suspension structural stiffness:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{susp}}'
  =
  25.61
  \]

  <p>
    gives:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{25.61}
  +
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'}
  =
  0.1111
  \]

  <p>
    Rearranging:
  </p>

  \[
  \frac{1}{K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'}
  =
  0.1111
  -
  \frac{1}{25.61}
  \]

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
  =
  \left(
  0.1111
  -
  \frac{1}{25.61}
  \right)^{-1}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
  \approx
  13.88
  }
  \]

  <p>
    Converting back to dimensional torsional stiffness:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  =
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
  K_{\mathrm{wheel}}
  \]

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  =
  13.88
  \left(
  171.92\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  \right)
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  \approx
  2386\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  }
  \]

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Target vs. Measured</h2>

  <p>
    The calculated minimum monocoque target is approximately:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  \approx
  2386\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  \]

  <p>
    The measured ARG26 monocoque stiffness was:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,measured}}
  =
  3364\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  \]

  <p>
    The stiffness margin relative to the target is:
  </p>

  \[
  \text{Stiffness margin}
  =
  \frac{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,measured}}
  -
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  }{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  }
  \]

  \[
  \text{Stiffness margin}
  =
  \frac{3364-2386}{2386}
  \]

  \[
  \boxed{
  \text{Stiffness margin}
  \approx
  41\%
  }
  \]

  <p>
    The measured ARG26 monocoque was approximately 41% stiffer than the
    calculated minimum target. This indicates that the structure provided a
    sufficiently rigid platform for the suspension, but also suggests that
    additional stiffness may have been achieved at the expense of unnecessary
    structural mass.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Analysis of Stiffness Margin</h2>

  <p>
    One contributor to the 41% stiffness margin was uncertainty in the composite
    material properties obtained from structural-equivalency testing.
  </p>

  <p>
    The three-point-bend test fixture used to characterize the composite panels
    contained more compliance than expected. This additional machine and fixture
    deflection caused the test specimens to appear more compliant than they
    actually were.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because the measured panel stiffness was artificially reduced, additional
    carbon-fiber plies were required in several regions to satisfy structural
    equivalency requirements. These more conservative ply schedules increased
    both the mass and torsional stiffness of the completed monocoque.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the upcoming testing campaign, new fixture designs are being implemented
    and benchmarked using materials with known elastic properties. The objective
    is to reduce test-rig compliance and improve confidence in the measured
    laminate properties.
  </p>

  <p>
    More accurate material characterization should allow future ply schedules to
    be sized closer to their actual structural requirements, reducing unnecessary
    mass while maintaining the calculated torsional stiffness target.
  </p>

</section>

