<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Conclusion</h2>

  <p>
    The analysis treats the suspension springs, suspension structure, and
    monocoque as torsional springs acting in series.
  </p>

  <p>
    The design objective is for 90% of total vehicle compliance to occur in the
    suspension springs. The monocoque and suspension structure are therefore
    permitted to contribute only the remaining 10% of total compliance.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is equivalent to requiring the combined structural stiffness of the
    monocoque and suspension structure to be approximately nine times greater
    than the equivalent torsional wheel rate.
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{structure}}
  =
  9K_{\mathrm{wheel}}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    Using a normalized wheel rate of 1, a measured normalized suspension
    structural stiffness of 25.61, and a target normalized vehicle stiffness of
    0.90, the required normalized monocoque stiffness was calculated as:
  </p>

  \[
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}'
  \approx
  13.88
  \]

  <p>
    This corresponds to a dimensional monocoque stiffness target of:
  </p>

  \[
  \boxed{
  K_{\mathrm{mono,target}}
  \approx
  2386\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}
  }
  \]

  <p>
    The measured monocoque stiffness of
    \(3364\ \mathrm{N\,m/deg}\) exceeded this requirement by approximately 41%,
    demonstrating that the ARG26 structure was sufficiently stiff relative to
    the suspension system.
  </p>

</section>

<section class="project-section">

  <h2>Assumptions and Limitations</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      The wheel rate, suspension structure, and monocoque are modeled as
      linear-elastic springs.
    </li>

    <li>
      The same torque is transmitted through each compliant component.
    </li>

    <li>
      Angular deflections from each component can be superimposed.
    </li>

    <li>
      Angular deflections are sufficiently small for the small-angle
      approximation to remain valid.
    </li>

    <li>
      The measured suspension structural stiffness is representative of the
      proposed vehicle architecture.
    </li>

    <li>
      Tire compliance, joint clearances, nonlinear deformation, and test-rig
      compliance are not explicitly included in the stiffness model.
    </li>

    <li>
      All stiffness values are expressed using consistent torsional units.
    </li>
  </ul>

